o":-71.20513,"d":495,"ds":"The Wolfe–Montcalm Monument is in Governors' Garden beyond the southern side of the Château Frontenac, Quebec. Completed in 1828, the obelisk is the oldest monument in Quebec City and the second-oldest war monument in Canada, the oldest being Nelson's Column in Montreal, which was completed in 1809.","wpu":"Wolfe–Montcalm Monument","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f8/Monument_Wolfe-Montcalm.jpg/960px-Monument_Wolfe-Montcalm.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Ursulines of Quebec","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":46.812094,"lo":-71.208127,"d":567,"ds":"The Ursuline Monastery of Quebec City (French: Monastère des Ursulines de Québec) was founded by a missionary group of Ursuline nuns in 1639 under the leadership of Mother Marie of the Incarnation, O.S.U. It is the oldest institution of learning for women in North America. Today, the monastery serves as the General Motherhouse of the Ursuline Sisters of the Canadian Union.","wpu":"Ursulines of Quebec","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/49/Couvent_des_Ursulines%2C_Qu%C3%A9bec.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled"},{"n":"St. Andrew's Church (Quebec City)","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":46.812964,"lo":-71.209661,"d":625,"ds":"St. Andrew's Presbyterian Church (French: Église presbytérienne Saint Andrew) is a Presbyterian church congregation located in Quebec City, Quebec, Canada. It belongs to the Presbyterian Church in Canada denomination.","wpu":"St. Andrew's Church (Quebec City)","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5b/St._Andrew%27s_Presbyterian_Church_Quebec_City.jpg/960px-St._Andrew%27s_Presbyterian_Church_Quebec_City.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Jesuit Chapel (Quebec City)","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":46.812134,"lo":-71.212265,"d":843,"ds":"The Jesuit Chapel is a chapel of the Society of Jesus located in the Old Quebec neighbourhood of Quebec City, Quebec, Canada. It was designed by François Baillairgé and built in from 1818 to 1930. It is situated on Rue Dauphine in Old Quebec close to the ramparts of Quebec City.","wpu":"Jesuit Chapel (Quebec City)","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c5/Chapelle_des_J%C3%A9suites_-_Quebec_City.jpg/960px-Chapelle_des_J%C3%A9suites_-_Quebec_City.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Place D'Youville (Quebec)","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":46.812222,"lo":-71.214167,"d":976,"ds":"Place D’Youville, also referred to as carré D'Youville, is a public square in the core of Quebec City, Quebec, Canada. It is situated on rue Saint-Jean, one of the oldest roads in Quebec City. It marks the boundary between the Quebec Parliament Hill and Old Quebec.","wpu":"Place D'Youville (Quebec)","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/91/Place_D%27Youville%2C_Qu%C3%A9bec%2C_Canada.jpg/960px-Place_D%27Youville%2C_Qu%C3%A9bec%2C_Canada.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Joseph-Samson Bridge–Tunnel","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":46.819444,"lo":-71.215278,"d":1124,"ds":"The Joseph Samson Bridge–Tunnel (French: Pont-Tunnel Joseph-Samson) is a vehicular bridge–tunnel in the Quebec City borough of La Cité-Limoilou.. It connects Capucin Boulevard in Old Limoilou with the old Port of Quebec. The bridge-tunnel first crosses over the Saint-Charles River at the north and then runs southeast, passing under the Gare du Palais train station and SAAQ headquarters (Jean Lesage Building).","wpu":"Joseph-Samson Bridge–Tunnel","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/92/Gare_du_Palais_-_Cote_Dinan.jpg/960px-Gare_du_Palais_-_Cote_Dinan.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Monument to the Suffragettes","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":46.8084,"lo":-71.21341,"d":1136,"ds":"The Monument to the Suffragettes is a public artwork located in Quebec City, Canada. The memorial is a sculpture of four key women in Quebec's political history: three suffragettes, Marie Lacoste Gérin-LaJoie, Idola Saint-Jean and Thérèse Forget-Casgrain; and Marie-Claire Kirkland, the first woman elected to the National Assembly.","wpu":"Monument to the Suffragettes","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a6/Monument_en_hommage_aux_femmes_en_politique_08.jpg/960px-Monument_en_hommage_aux_femmes_en_politique_08.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Édifice André-Laurendeau","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":46.8084,"lo":-71.2155,"d":1262,"ds":"Édifice André-Laurendeau (French pronunciation: [edifis ɑ̃dʁe lɔʁɑ̃do]) is an eleven-storey office tower located at 1050, rue des Parlementaires in Quebec City, Quebec. The Beaux-Arts structure was built between 1935 and 1937, designed by Lacroix, Drouin and Bergeron, and is the property of the Crown in Right of Quebec. In 1980, it was named in honour of journalist and politician André Laurendeau.","wpu":"Édifice André-Laurendeau","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bb/%C3%89difice_Andr%C3%A9-Laurendeau.jpg/960px-%C3%89difice_Andr%C3%A9-Laurendeau.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Saint-Jean-Baptiste Church (Quebec City)","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":46.809444,"lo":-71.222222,"d":1658,"ds":"Saint-Jean-Baptiste Church was a church in Quebec City, Quebec located at 410 Rue Saint-Jean.","wpu":"Saint-Jean-Baptiste Church (Quebec City)","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fb/Eglise_Saint-Jean-Baptiste_Quebec.JPG/960px-Eglise_Saint-Jean-Baptiste_Quebec.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"}],"about":{"ds":"Quebec City (French: Ville de Québec) is the capital city of the Canadian province of Quebec. As of July 2021, the city had a population of 549,459 and the Quebec City census metropolitan area (including surrounding communities) had a population of 839,311. It is the twelfth-largest city and the seventh-largest metropolitan area in Canada.","wpu":"Quebec City","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a4/Quebec_City_skyline_from_L%C3%A9vis.jpg/960px-Quebec_City_skyline_from_L%C3%A9vis.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},"wk":"2026-10-18"}